# 0039 - a leftover in the receive ring is cleared, never replayed; and the copy that
#        becomes one goes with its message.
#
# S1-II (2026-09-19), found in three evening runs of the 8+1 rig (runs/split174430-9,
# runs/split175954-9, runs/split185900-9): in each, one hand stalled for a minute or more
# because ONE seat lacked a message every other seat had -- the rest of the table moved on
# and waited for that seat, that seat waited for the sender, seven clocks ran out on it,
# and the table's own repairs (D-033, D-059, D-065) ended the hand. The seat was not deaf:
# its counters for every peer went on rising through the whole stall.
#
# The carrier keeps each connection's messages in rings of GCC_BUFFER_SIZE = 2048 slots,
# slot = id % 2048. Two things, both upstream's:
#
#   1. gcc_check_recv_array, the periodic drain, looked at the SLOT of the awaited message
#      and took whatever was in it for the awaited message. It never compared the id.
#   2. A message that arrives out of order is stored; if its retransmission then arrives in
#      order before the drain has run, the message is handed straight to its handler and
#      the stored copy is never cleared -- the drain only ever looks at the awaited slot,
#      and that has just moved past it.
#
# So a copy left behind by (2) sat in its slot for a lap of the ring, and 2048 messages
# later (1) replayed it: handed it to the application a second time, acknowledged it under
# its OLD id, and moved received_message_id past a message that had not arrived. The real
# one was then a duplicate by its number -- dropped, and acknowledged, so the sender forgot
# it as well. A lossless stream lost one message, to one receiver, with no error anywhere.
#
# This project's own repairs made (2) systematic, which is why upstream rarely meets it: a
# receiver asks at once for what it misses (0009) and a sender answers with a run of
# consecutive ids (0024), so after every reordering the retransmitted run arrives in one
# poll batch and overtakes every copy stored ahead of it.
#
# The sender's half was in the logs all along, as upstream's own line: the ack under the
# old id finds another message in that slot of the SEND ring, and gcc_handle_ack says
# "Wrap-around on message N". Measured in those three runs: 2, 109 and 205 such lines with
# N above zero; N always small (123..776, the first lap); none in the first 250 s of any
# run, and none before some connection's counter had passed 2048; every one at a moment
# when a receiver's counter passed N + 2048 (no other offset fits them all: +1024 fits
# 0 to 12 %, +4096 0 to 4 %); in runs of consecutive ids, which is 0024's burst.
#
# The change, in toxcore/group_connection.c:
#   * gcc_handle_received_message: when the awaited message arrives in order and a copy
#     of the same id waits in the ring, the copy is cleared with it (and said, at DEBUG).
#   * gcc_check_recv_array: an occupant of the awaited slot whose id is not the awaited id
#     is a leftover by construction -- everything below has been consumed, and a sender
#     cannot be 2048 ahead of what this side has handled, its own ring holding 2047
#     unacknowledged messages and no more -- so it is cleared and said, never processed.
#     That also closes the door to a member who SENDS awaited + 2048 on purpose.
#
# Measured after, the same rig the same evening (runs/split195458-9 and the runs that
# followed): "Wrap-around" with N above zero 0 lines; the copy cleared with its message
# 4 475 times in one seven-minute run, which is how many leftovers a run was making (only
# those of the first few hundred ids come round inside seven minutes; at a table played
# for an hour all of them do); the drain's own check never had to fire. Read a run with
# tools/fold-ring-leftovers.py.
#
# Local; the wire is untouched. The DEBUG lines are compiled in under fault-harness only
# (build.rs sets MIN_LOGGER_LEVEL there and nowhere else).
#
# Vendored tree patched in place. Re-apply on a toxcore bump with
#   git apply patches/0039-*.patch
# and check tools/build-tox.ps1's marker for 0039 in group_connection.c.
diff --git a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_connection.c b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_connection.c
index 8b63d17..b305b99 100644
--- a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_connection.c
+++ b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/group_connection.c
@@ -620,6 +620,36 @@ int gcc_handle_received_message(const Logger *log, const Memory *mem, const Mono
         return 1;
     }
 
+    /* p2p-poker (patch 0039): a copy that waited in the ring goes with the message.
+     *
+     * This message is the awaited one and is handed straight to its handler; it
+     * never touches recv_array. But a COPY of it may be there: it arrived once
+     * before, out of order, and was stored -- and what arrives now is the
+     * sender's retransmission, which won the race with the periodic drain
+     * (gcc_check_recv_array runs once a do_gc pass; a poll delivers a whole
+     * batch before it). Nothing ever cleared that copy: the drain looks at the
+     * slot of received_message_id + 1 only, and that has just moved past it.
+     * The leftover then sat in its slot for a lap of the ring, and see
+     * gcc_check_recv_array for what it did when the ids came round.
+     *
+     * Made systematic by this project's own repairs, which is why upstream
+     * rarely meets it: a receiver asks at once for what it misses (0009) and a
+     * sender answers with a run of consecutive ids (0024), so after every
+     * reordering the retransmitted run arrives in one batch and overtakes every
+     * copy stored ahead of it. Measured, three runs of nine seats on
+     * 2026-09-19: 2, 109 and 205 leftovers a run, in runs of consecutive ids.
+     */
+    {
+        const uint16_t idx = gcc_get_array_index(message_id);
+        GC_Message_Array_Entry *const copy = &gconn->recv_array[idx];
+
+        if (!array_entry_is_empty(copy) && copy->message_id == message_id) {
+            LOGGER_DEBUG(log, "p2p-poker: message %llu arrived in order while a copy of it waited in the ring; the copy goes with it",
+                         (unsigned long long)message_id);
+            clear_array_entry(mem, copy);
+        }
+    }
+
     gcc_set_recv_message_id(gconn, gconn->received_message_id + 1);
 
     return 2;
@@ -707,9 +737,46 @@ void gcc_check_recv_array(const GC_Session *c, GC_Chat *chat, GC_Connection *gco
     const uint16_t idx = (gconn->received_message_id + 1) % GCC_BUFFER_SIZE;
     GC_Message_Array_Entry *const array_entry = &gconn->recv_array[idx];
 
-    if (!array_entry_is_empty(array_entry)) {
-        process_recv_array_entry(c, chat, gconn, peer_number, array_entry, userdata);
+    if (array_entry_is_empty(array_entry)) {
+        return;
     }
+
+    /* p2p-poker (patch 0039): a leftover in the receive ring is cleared, never replayed.
+     *
+     * This looked at the SLOT of the awaited message and took whatever was in
+     * it for the awaited message. A slot is `id % GCC_BUFFER_SIZE`, so an entry
+     * left behind 2048 messages ago has the same slot -- and was handed to the
+     * application a second time, acknowledged under its OLD id, and counted as
+     * the awaited message: received_message_id moved past a message that had
+     * not arrived. When the real one came it was a duplicate by its number,
+     * dropped and acknowledged, so the sender forgot it too. **A lossless
+     * stream lost one message, to one receiver, with no error on either side.**
+     *
+     * The sender's half of it is a line upstream already prints: the ack under
+     * the old id finds another message in that slot of the send ring, and
+     * gcc_handle_ack says "Wrap-around on message N" -- with N small, from the
+     * first lap, and only once that connection has carried N + 2048 messages.
+     * Measured on 2026-09-19, three runs of nine seats, 316 such lines: every
+     * one came after some receiver's counter had passed N + 2048 (no other
+     * offset fits them all), none in the first 250 s of any run, and in each
+     * run one hand stalled for a minute or more on a seat that lacked a message
+     * every other seat had (S1-II).
+     *
+     * A different id here is a leftover by construction. Everything below the
+     * awaited id has been consumed, and a sender cannot be 2048 ahead of what
+     * this side has handled -- its own ring holds 2047 unacknowledged messages
+     * and no more -- so no message still to come shares this slot with the
+     * awaited one.
+     */
+    if (array_entry->message_id != gconn->received_message_id + 1) {
+        LOGGER_DEBUG(chat->log, "p2p-poker: a leftover in the receive ring is cleared, not replayed: slot %u held message %llu while %llu is awaited",
+                     idx, (unsigned long long)array_entry->message_id,
+                     (unsigned long long)(gconn->received_message_id + 1));
+        clear_array_entry(chat->mem, array_entry);
+        return;
+    }
+
+    process_recv_array_entry(c, chat, gconn, peer_number, array_entry, userdata);
 }
 
 void gcc_resend_packets(const GC_Chat *chat, GC_Connection *gconn)
